 \begin{abstract}
-Geometry-conditioned multi-view diffusion provides an inference-time strength control, but its practical effect depends on temporal allocation, the conditioning interface, and the evaluated output. We conduct a controlled empirical study of Timestep-Conditioned Adapter Scaling (TCAS), carrying forward the historical middle-high profile C3 without further schedule selection. On 300 held-out objects with a frozen retrained GeoTex checkpoint, C3 improves foreground PSNR by 0.52 dB over fixed low, while competitive fixed and linear controls have better means on all four image endpoints. Matching applied-scale budgets changes the interpretation: C3 is 0.13 dB below fixed mean and 0.56 dB below late-high. Native MV-Adapter studies on 50 Fresh C inputs and 100 GSO objects detect endpoint-specific stage effects but do not detect the primary middle-window benefit over fixed mean. A ten-object native-UV bake pilot finds a small late-high LPIPS reduction of 0.00265 on unseen views; a separate twenty-object GSO evaluation with revised calibration does not detect this benefit. Generated-view and baked-output method rankings remain positively associated, although the preferred point estimates often differ. Fixed-seed and shared-UV diagnostics distinguish group-average gains, objectwise repeatability and incoming-view consistency from fidelity. These findings quantify application-specific effects and limits of geometry-control allocation. They support neither a universally preferred schedule nor physically faithful materials.
+Geometry-conditioned multi-view diffusion exposes inference-time strength controls, but their value depends on competitive baselines, implemented budgets and evaluation endpoints. We study geometry-control allocation using the historical Timestep-Conditioned Adapter Scaling (TCAS) profile C3 without further schedule selection. On 300 held-out objects with a frozen retrained GeoTex checkpoint, equal-budget C3 is 0.13 dB below fixed mean and 0.56 dB below late-high in foreground PSNR. The addendum's internally specified middle-minus-average-of-early-and-late contrast is not detected: $-0.034$ dB, 95\% interval $[-0.117,+0.051]$. C3's 0.52 dB gain over fixed low changes strength as well as timing; competitive fixed and linear controls have better means on all four image endpoints. A separate retrained technical panel requested on the historical 276 UIDs illustrates endpoint dependence: C3 exceeds fixed high by 1.70 dB in full-frame PSNR but trails by 1.31 dB in foreground PSNR. One failed input was randomly substituted; excluding its requested slot preserves both directions, and this panel is not exact held-out confirmation. Native MV-Adapter evaluations on 50 Fresh C inputs and 100 GSO objects do not detect the specified middle-window benefit. Actual baking finds a small late-high LPIPS benefit on ten compatible GeoTex objects and no detected benefit on a separate twenty-object GSO cohort. Fixed-seed and shared-UV diagnostics distinguish average effects, objectwise repeatability and incoming-view consistency from fidelity. The findings support application-specific evaluation, rather than a universally preferred schedule or faithful material reproduction.
 \end{abstract}
 
